Name: appAppearance

Type: property

Syntax: set the appAppearance to {"light" | "dark" | "system"}

Syntax: get the [effective] appAppearance

Summary:
Specifies whether the application is drawn in the light or the dark
appearance, or follows the operating system's setting.

Introduced: 9.7.1

OS: mac, windows, linux

Platforms: desktop

Example:
-- In the startup or preOpenStack handler of a standalone's mainstack:
-- follow the dark mode of Windows and macOS
set the appAppearance to "system"

Example:
set the appAppearance to "dark"
put the effective appAppearance -- "dark" on Windows and macOS

Example:
-- Feature test, for scripts that also run on older engines
try
   do "get the appAppearance"
   put true into tHasAppearance
catch tError
   put false into tHasAppearance
end try

Value:
The <appAppearance> is "light", "dark" or "system". Setting it is not
case-sensitive; getting it returns lower case. By default it is "light",
in every engine: the IDE, standalones, the command line (-ui) and the
server. It is not saved: each launch starts at "light". Setting it to any
other value is an error, and leaves it as it was.

The <effective> <appAppearance> is "light" or "dark": the <appAppearance>
with "system" resolved to the operating system's setting (the
<systemAppearance>). It is "light" while a Windows High Contrast theme is
on, because the dark appearance does not use its colors.

Description:
Use the <appAppearance> to choose the appearance the engine draws
<stack|stacks> in when they do not choose their own with the
<stackAppearance>.

In the dark appearance every color a stack leaves unset comes from the
dark theme: white text, dark fills, dark native controls, scrollbars and
title bars. Colors a script or the author set are always drawn as set.
Where the author set some colors and not others (a white field without a
text color, for example), the engine fits the unset colors to the colors
around the object, so a stack designed light still looks as designed in
the dark appearance; see the <stackAppearance>.

Standalones are light unless they opt in. Windows and macOS let each
application decide whether it follows dark mode, and every stack made
before this property existed was designed light. To follow the system,
put one line in the startup or preOpenStack handler of the mainstack:

    set the appAppearance to "system"

Standalones built with OpenXTalk Lite 1.14 or later, or with OXT-Beyond
0.1.0 or earlier, followed the dark mode of Windows (and of macOS). Built
again with this engine they are light unless they add that line.

Setting the <appAppearance> redraws every window at once in its new
appearance. It does not send <systemAppearanceChanged>, which is only sent
when the operating system's setting changes.

On Linux the native controls are drawn by the GTK theme, which the engine
cannot change: the <effective> <appAppearance> and the appearance of
every stack are the GTK theme's (the <systemAppearance>), whatever the
<appAppearance> is set to. The setting is still stored and returned.

Printing is always done in the light appearance. Snapshots
(<export snapshot>, <import snapshot>) are taken in the appearance the
objects are shown in; to export a light image from a dark stack, set the
<appAppearance> (or the <stackAppearance>) to "light" around the export.

Changes:
The <appAppearance> was added in OXT-Beyond after release 0.1.0 (engine
9.7.1-OXT). Before it, the engine always followed the dark mode of Windows
and macOS.

References: stackAppearance (property), systemAppearance (property),
systemAppearanceChanged (message), effective (keyword),
export snapshot (command), import snapshot (command), stack (object)

Tags: ui
